  <!-- ═══ 13 · AN IMPORTANT DISTINCTION: deep anchor ═══ -->
  <section class="cfm-dark relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none" style="background: radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 0%, hsl(42 40% 30% / 0.1), transparent 55%);"></div>
    <div class="relative container mx-auto px-6 py-20 md:py-28 max-w-3xl cfm-reveal">
      <h2 class="font-heading text-gold-gradient text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-[44px] leading-tight mb-12">An important distinction</h2>
      <div class="font-body text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-7 max-w-[65ch] mb-12">
        <p>Some experiences that arise through practice are difficult to understand, especially when they involve marked changes in perception, identity, emotion, energy, or the ordinary rhythms of daily life. In the face of that, people understandably look for certainty. Sometimes it arrives as a clinical explanation; sometimes as a spiritual one. Both can be valuable, and both can become limiting when they are reached for too quickly or held too tightly.</p>
        <p class="cfm-emph">The honest position is that a human life rarely fits a single explanation.</p>
        <p>There are times when psychological, psychiatric, or medical support is essential and can make a real difference to a person's wellbeing. There are also times when the contemplative traditions offer context that helps someone feel less frightened, less alone, and more able to meet what is happening. These possibilities are not necessarily opposed.</p>
        <p>Part of this work is making space for that kind of careful reflection: so that support is sought where it is needed, assumptions are questioned where that helps, and an experience is understood as fully as it can be. This is not a stance against therapy, psychiatry, or medicine, and it does not assume that every unusual experience is spiritual. It simply holds that experiences deserve to be looked at thoughtfully rather than reduced too soon to a single answer.</p>
        <p>Where specialist support is needed, I will say so directly, and help point you towards it.</p>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-gold-light/50 pl-7">
        <p class="font-heading cfm-gold-light-text text-2xl md:text-3xl italic leading-relaxed">Understanding begins the moment we are willing to look more carefully than our first explanation allows.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <div class="mt-10 pt-8 border-t border-parchment/15">
        <p class="font-body text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.14em] cfm-gold-light-text mb-4">Related reading</p>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 font-body text-sm">
          <a href="/wisdom/can-meditation-make-anxiety-or-dissociation-worse" class="cfm-link text-parchment/80 w-fit">Can meditation make anxiety or dissociation worse? &rarr;</a>
          <a href="/wisdom/meditation-experiences-when-to-pause-and-seek-support" class="cfm-link text-parchment/80 w-fit">Meditation experiences: when to pause and seek support &rarr;</a>
          <a href="/wisdom/spiritual-emergence-psychological-distress-or-both" class="cfm-link text-parchment/80 w-fit">Spiritual emergence, psychological distress, or both? &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══ 14 · WHAT THIS IS NOT ═══ -->
  <section class="cfm-warm-bg">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-20 max-w-2xl cfm-reveal">
      <h2 class="font-heading cfm-gold-text text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-tight mb-8">What this is not</h2>
      <div class="font-body text-base md:text-lg text-foreground/72 leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p>This work is not psychotherapy, psychiatric treatment, diagnosis, or medical care, and it is not a substitute for professional mental health support where that is needed. Where I believe specialist care would help, I will say so directly.</p>
        <p>It is not a guru-disciple relationship. My role is not to tell you what to believe, what your experiences mean, or which path to follow. I do not offer final answers, or a system of interpretation into which every experience must fit.</p>
        <p>What I offer is a reflective counselling space: somewhere experiences, questions, and insights can be looked at carefully and honestly, so that your own understanding has room to emerge. The aim is not dependence. It is greater clarity, grounding, and freedom in your relationship with your own experience.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

